at launch there are two options when it  comes to new generation xbox consoles  the more powerful and equipped xbox  series x or the more affordable but  somewhat limited xbox series  s i've already delivered my review of  the series x but what does the series s  offer well it's a cute and compact  console that'll play next gen xbox games  and for a relatively cheap entry price  of 300 bucks  it's a sleek system that comes with many  of the same great features of the  beefier series x  with some compromises that you'll need  to consider let's first talk about  performance the rdna 2 gpu and zen 2 cpu  from amd that are packed into the series  s  are scaled down versions of what's in  the series x it's all part of the same  tech architecture  microsoft's aim for the series s is to  hit 1440p  resolution instead of full 4k which is  still ambitious all things considered  and when playing optimized games on the  smaller console  it's genuinely impressive gears 5 looks  and runs great  it might not have the same clarity and  the details might not shine through  as distinctly as they do on the series x  but you still get a consistent 60fps  at what looks to be 1440p the series s  also has room to do 120 fps if  optimizations allow for it  and you have the proper 120hz display  this is the case in gears 5 multiplayer  forza horizon 4 is noteworthy as well  since it can hit 60fps  and maintain its fantastic visual flare  at what looks to be a solid 1440p  resolution on a 4k tv  i can tell the image isn't as sharp as  it is on the series x but otherwise it  looks crisp  one curious case is yakuza like a dragon  as of now it actually offers  the same two graphics options as it does  on the series x albeit scaled down for  the series s hardware  high resolution mode seems to do 1440p  30fps  and normal mode looks to be 1080p at a  smooth 60fps  this could mean that we may see similar  options pop up even for series s  optimized games in the future as for  backwards compatibility the series  s essentially takes base xbox one builds  of games  and goes from there this has an impact  on what you get out of backwards  compatible games compared to the series  x  if a game is not optimized for next gen  the series x will play the  1x enhanced version however the series s  will only play the base  xbox one version meaning you'll miss out  on the enhancements that came from the  previous generation's high-end console  for example let's look at final fantasy  xv on series x you can  access the 1x enhancements which allow  you to choose to boost frame rate or  crank up the resolution  when playing on series s you simply get  the base xbox one version  that runs at a capped 30fps with its  blurry sub 1080p resolution  doom eternal is 1x enhanced and runs  great on the series x2  but it's a bit disappointing to play the  xbox one version on the series s  which caps at 900p resolution to  maintain 60fps  the series s can run back and pack games  with more stable performance  but as of now it's held back by the base  xbox one builds of games in these cases  the reasons for this are understandable  but the series s has so much power left  over  so it'll be up to developers to go back  and implement optimizations if they want  so things could improve  while the series s concedes some  graphical horsepower it doesn't skimp  out on the ssd  in terms of speed it packs the same type  of internal storage drive as the series  x so you get the same ultra fast load  times  some examples i've used in previous  coverage are final fantasy xv  the outer worlds and control which all  load from the main menu  and into gameplay in about 10 to 12  seconds yakuza like a dragon and gears 5  only take about 5 to 6  seconds the xbox one x internal drive  would take about 4 to 5 times longer  for these games to me this is a real  game changer  a practical and tangible benefit that  makes the gaming experience  closer to seamless once you get a taste  of super fast loading in games you will  not want to go back to anything lesser  where the series s makes a major cut is  in its ssd's  size the 512 gigabyte internal drive  actually comes out to 364 gigabytes  of free usable space considering the  size of games  this is not a lot of storage if you play  all of call of duty's modes well  that's a third of your drive gone now  some games actually have smaller install  sizes  specifically for the series s forza  horizon 4 takes up 71.4 gigabytes on the  series s  as opposed to 84.6 gigabytes on the  series x  and gears 5 takes up 55.1 gigabytes  down from 71.9 gigabytes this helps  somewhat but not by much  however sea of thieves chops off a lot  down to 17 gigabytes  from 46.6 gigabytes developers can  decide which assets are part of the  installation  so mileage may vary you have some  options for expanding storage though the  one terabyte seagate drive is  technically the best solution but it's  also the most expensive  at 220 bucks you'd be paying more than  you would for a series x  if you're gonna pair that with a series  s you can use external usb 3 drives to  play backwards compatible games and the  load times can be pretty quick on  external ssds  however you can't play next gen or  series s optimized games straight off  the drive  you have to transfer the install files  to internal storage which thankfully  doesn't take that long  either way if you want to move beyond  that 364 gigabyte limitation  and you don't already have an external  drive it's going to cost you  most other things stay the same in terms  of the user experience  the ui is the same it's as snappy as it  is on the series x  and quick resume works exactly as it's  intended as i've said before  suspending up to 5 or 6 games and  jumping between them in a matter of  seconds  is pretty sweet and a useful feature and  the fact that games stay suspended after  powering off the system  is cool too as we move more toward  digital distribution  the lack of a disk drive on the series s  might not be all that bad  especially considering the emphasis on  game pass for xbox's future  but those who kept their physical xbox  one and 360 games  will be out of luck with the series s or  if you use your system for entertainment  on blu-ray if that's important to you  the series s  is a no-go whereas the series x has a  sort of low-key yet imposing presence to  its design  the series s is cute maybe you think  it's a plain white box with a big fan  vent on top but considering the power it  packs and the fact that it's a next-gen  system  it's impressive it's tiny light and  sports a minimalist aesthetic  and it's literally the smallest xbox  ever overall it's nice that you can  access next-gen for 300 bucks  with a compact box like the series s an  entry level option is just cool to see  out there  moving forward games will naturally  accommodate the series s  so impressive cases like forza verizon 4  and gears 5  will be more common whether or not this  should be your system of choice for next  gen is determined by how much its  compromises affect you  and also if you care about 4k if those  things are important to you  consider saving a bit more for a series  x the series s could serve as a great  cost-effective system  that is complementary to say a  playstation or switch  if you just want to access what the  platform offers now or what's to come in  xbox's future  and if you don't mind the graphical and  storage gap this is the xbox for you  [Music]
 

